C = [
("h1", "Preface"),
("p", "This volume argues a position. It does not gather what churches say about signs and leave the reader to choose. It says what the author believes the Scriptures teach about the signs, wonders and miracles of the Old and New Testaments, it sets the verses in the order they stand, and it answers the strongest objections in the objectors’ own words, so that a reader who disagrees knows where the disagreement lies."),
("p", "The question is narrow. Scripture uses one set of words for what God does to mark a messenger, to move a nation and to answer a doubter: sign, wonder, miracle, token. The same words are used when Moses faces Pharaoh, when the Lord stands in Galilee, when Peter stands in Solomon’s porch and when Paul lands on Malta. The volume asks three things of those words. What was a sign for? To whom was it given? And what, in the book of Acts, is the last sign the text records?"),
("p", "The answers are taken from the passages themselves. A sign in Scripture is given to someone and for something. The Lord says to Moses that the signs are given “that they may believe that the LORD God of their fathers … hath appeared unto thee” (Exodus 4:5). Paul writes that “the Jews require a sign” (1 Corinthians 1:22). Peter tells the men of Israel that Jesus was “approved of God among you by miracles and wonders and signs” (Acts 2:22). Each sentence names a giver, a hearer and a purpose, and the volume reads every sign in the same way: who gave it, who received it, and what it was set to prove."),
("p", "The position is this. The signs, wonders and miracles of Scripture belong to a program: God’s dealing with Israel by messengers whose words He confirmed. They were given to Israel and to the nations in their contact with Israel. They accompanied the Lord, the Twelve and the men who spoke with them. The record of signs in Acts runs through the overlap of chapters 9 to 28 and ends in chapter 28 with the healings on Malta. After the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program at that chapter, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program."),
("p", "Three things about the method. First, the volume reads the King James Version only, and every Scripture quotation is checked word for word against it. Second, each chapter opens with a claim, reads its passages in order, and closes with a short table and a list of what the volume takes from the passages, so that the reader can see what has been argued and test it. Third, where a modern writer is named, the volume says what that writer holds, and gives the writer’s words only where it has read them in a free full text."),
("p", "The volume does not take up what its neighbours argue. Mark 16:9–20 and the textual question that attends it belong to Study 25. The Spirit’s coming at Pentecost and at Ephesus belongs to Acts Commentary Volume 2, the close of Acts and the national suspension to Acts Commentary Volume 5, and Romans 15:18, 19 to Gospel Commentary Volume 2. The tongues of 1 Corinthians 12 to 14 belong to Volume 2 of this series, healing to Volume 3, and the apostles and prophets to Volume 4. This volume cites those places and does not repeat them. It also leaves aside what the text does not state: how a sign was done, how much each witness understood, and what God may do apart from the programs the text describes."),
("p", "One boundary runs through the volume. Paul’s signs and those of his companions in Acts 9 to 28 are reported as the text reports them. The volume does not assign them to Israel’s program and does not convert them into the Mystery. They stand in settings the book of Acts describes, and the volume reads them in those settings."),
("h1", "Thesis"),
("p", "Signs in Scripture are given to a person, for a purpose, and the purpose is stated in the passage. The signs of the Old Testament and the Gospels, and the signs of the Twelve and of Paul in Acts, were given to Israel and its messengers to confirm what God was saying. The record in Acts ends at chapter 28. With the national suspension of Israel’s Prophecy Program, the sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program."),
("ctbl", "The thesis in four statements.", [
    ("Statement", "What it says", "Where it is argued"),
    ("1", "A sign is given to a hearer for a purpose the passage states. Some signs are not miracles at all: the bow in the cloud, circumcision and the sabbath are each called a token or a sign.", "Chapters 1 and 2"),
    ("2", "The Old Testament signs confirm a messenger to Israel and to the nations that deal with Israel, and the Lord’s signs and the Twelve’s signs confirm the same message in the same line.", "Chapters 3 to 6"),
    ("3", "The record of signs in Acts runs through chapters 2 to 28, the signs of Paul and his companions are reported as the text reports them, and the last sign recorded is the healing on Malta.", "Chapters 7 to 10"),
    ("4", "The sign-program associated with Israel ceases programmatically at the national suspension of Acts 28, and signs do not continue into the Mystery Program. The passages on gifts and tongues in Paul’s letters are argued in Volumes 2 and 3.", "Chapters 9 to 11"),
  ], [0.15, 0.60, 0.25]),
("p", "A reader may test the thesis in one move. Take any passage that uses the word “sign” and ask three questions of it: who gave the sign, who received it, and what the text says it was for. The volume holds that each passage answers all three and that, in the passages read here, the answers run through Israel and the messengers sent to it. If a passage can be produced where they do not, the thesis fails at that passage. The volume reads every passage it knows that bears on the question, and it states plainly where a passage belongs to a later volume."),
]
